#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Send a claude-watch QUEUE push notification via Pushover.

This is the dedicated delivery path for session-task queue lifecycle
notifications (queue started / done / abandoned / force-start / blocked,
etc.). It deliberately does NOT go through ``pingme``:

  * It uses a SEPARATE Pushover application/API token so queue pushes are
    distinguishable from pingme's general-purpose pushes, and can carry a
    dedicated default sound.
  * It REUSES the existing Pushover *user* key (the same recipient pingme
    pushes to) so notifications still land on the same device(s).
  * It delivers quietly by default (``sound=vibrate``).

Drop-in argv compatibility
---------------------------
session-task previously shelled out to ``pingme -p <priority> <message>
<title>``. This tool accepts the same shape so the session-task call site
needed only a binary-name swap:

    queue-notify [-p PRIORITY] [--sound SOUND] <message> [title]

``PRIORITY`` is one of ``low|normal|high|emergency`` (mapped to Pushover
priority -1/0/1/2). ``--sound`` overrides the default ``vibrate``.

Debounce + batch (coalesce bursts into ONE push)
------------------------------------------------
Queue lifecycle transitions fire in bursts — a `/clear`, a wave of agent
completions, or a group of items registering/finishing together can emit
a dozen `_pingme_notify` calls within seconds, and (pre-2026-08) each one
became its own Pushover, spamming the recipient's phone.

To fix that WITHOUT touching session-task's ~dozen per-event call sites,
the debounce+batch layer lives HERE, between "invoked N times" and "POST
once". session-task still invokes ``queue-notify`` once per transition
(the historical contract, still exercised by the pingme test-suite); this
tool spools each invocation and coalesces a burst into a SINGLE summarised
push:

    "5 queue events: 2 done, 2 started, 1 abandoned
     • done q-... — <summary>
     • started q-... — <summary>
     ..."

Mechanism (works across ephemeral CLI invocations — there is no long-lived
process to hold a timer):

  * Each invocation APPENDS a JSON record to a spool file (flock-guarded)
    and spawns a detached "flusher" subprocess (``queue-notify
    --__flush-spool``). Only the FIRST flusher wins a non-blocking lock;
    the rest exit immediately, so a burst spawns exactly one live flusher.
  * The flusher DEBOUNCES: it waits until no new event has landed for
    ``QUEUE_NOTIFY_DEBOUNCE`` seconds (a quiet gap), bounded by a hard
    ``QUEUE_NOTIFY_MAX_WINDOW`` ceiling so a continuous stream can't starve
    the flush. Then it drains the spool and sends ONE push (a lone event
    is sent verbatim; ≥2 are summarised).

Batching is skipped (immediate synchronous send, legacy behaviour) when:
  * ``QUEUE_NOTIFY_BATCH=0`` (kill switch / tests), or
  * priority is ``emergency`` — a priority-2 alert with retry/expire must
    never sit in a debounce window.

Transition push policy (which transitions are worth a phone push)
-----------------------------------------------------------------
Not every queue lifecycle transition deserves to buzz a phone. ``started``
and ``done`` are pure BOOKKEEPING: they fire on every register and every
completion, they carry no decision for the operator, and the same
information is already on the queue minisite and in the claude-event
stream. In Aug 2026 those two transitions alone accounted for ~3,000
Pushover messages in three weeks — about 31% of the account's monthly
message allowance — spent entirely on "a thing started" / "a thing
finished" notices nobody acts on.

So this tool applies a POLICY before the debounce/batch layer: only
transitions in the push set produce a Pushover. The default set is every
known lifecycle transition EXCEPT ``started`` and ``done`` — i.e. the ones
that actually ask something of the operator or report a failure:

    abandoned, blocked, force-started, quarantined, unblocked, unwedged,
    wedged

Two properties matter:

  * The claude-event emission is UNAFFECTED. session-task emits
    ``queue-started`` / ``queue-done`` claude-events exactly as before, so
    the main loop, the minisite and the exporter see no change at all.
    Only the phone push is dropped.
  * The filter FAILS OPEN on anything it does not recognise as a known
    lifecycle transition (a hand-written title, or a transition kind added
    to session-task later). A new transition pushes until someone
    deliberately adds it to the suppressed set — silence is never the
    default for something unknown.

Override with ``QUEUE_NOTIFY_PUSH_TRANSITIONS`` (comma/space separated,
case-insensitive; a leading ``queue `` on each entry is tolerated):

    QUEUE_NOTIFY_PUSH_TRANSITIONS=all          # push everything (pre-2026-08)
    QUEUE_NOTIFY_PUSH_TRANSITIONS=none         # push nothing known
    QUEUE_NOTIFY_PUSH_TRANSITIONS=wedged,blocked
    QUEUE_NOTIFY_PUSH_TRANSITIONS=default,done # the default set plus `done`

``default`` expands to the built-in set, so re-enabling ONE transition
does not mean re-listing all of them. A suppressed transition exits 0 —
it is a policy decision, not a delivery failure.

Env knobs (all optional):
  * ``QUEUE_NOTIFY_PUSH_TRANSITIONS`` — transitions that earn a push
    (default: all known lifecycle transitions except ``started`` /
    ``done``). ``all`` / ``none`` / ``default`` are accepted tokens.
  * ``QUEUE_NOTIFY_BATCH``       — ``0`` disables batching (default on).
  * ``QUEUE_NOTIFY_DEBOUNCE``    — quiet-gap seconds (default 10).
  * ``QUEUE_NOTIFY_MAX_WINDOW``  — hard coalesce ceiling seconds (default 60).
  * ``QUEUE_NOTIFY_SPOOL``       — spool file path (default
    ``$XDG_CACHE_HOME/queue-notify/spool.jsonl``).
  * ``QUEUE_NOTIFY_SINK``        — dry-run: append the composed delivery as
    a JSON line to this file INSTEAD of POSTing (tests / debugging).

Credential lookup
-----------------
Token (dedicated, queue-only):
  1. ``QUEUE_NOTIFY_TOKEN`` / ``PUSHOVER_TOKEN`` already in env.
  2. ``$QUEUE_NOTIFY_ENV`` file (default
     ``$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/queue-notify/env``, chmod 600) → ``PUSHOVER_TOKEN``.

User key (reused from pingme — same recipient):
  1. ``PUSHOVER_USER`` already in env.
  2. ``PUSHOVER_USER`` set in the queue-notify env file (explicit override).
  3. ``PUSHOVER_USER`` sourced from pingme's env file
     (``$PINGME_ENV`` / ``$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/pingme/env``).

The token NEVER lives in this (public-repo) file — only in the local
``queue-notify/env`` secret. If creds can't be found, this exits non-zero
with a clear message; callers (session-task) swallow that so the queue op
is never blocked by a notification failure.
"""
import argparse
import fcntl
import json
import os
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import time
import urllib.error
import urllib.parse
import urllib.request
from collections import Counter
from pathlib import Path

API_URL = "https://api.pushover.net/1/messages.json"
DEFAULT_SOUND = "vibrate"

_PRIORITY_MAP = {"low": -1, "normal": 0, "high": 1, "emergency": 2}
_PRIORITY_REV = {v: k for k, v in _PRIORITY_MAP.items()}

# Debounce/batch defaults (seconds).
_DEFAULT_DEBOUNCE = 10.0
_DEFAULT_MAX_WINDOW = 60.0
# Cap on how many per-event detail lines the batched message spells out.
_BATCH_DETAIL_CAP = 12
# Keep the composed body under Pushover's 1024-char message limit.
_MESSAGE_LIMIT = 1000

# --- transition push policy ------------------------------------------------
#
# Every queue lifecycle transition session-task can emit, as the lower-cased
# `kind` half of a "queue <kind>: q-..." title. Anything NOT in this set is
# treated as an unknown/ad-hoc title and always pushes (fail open).
KNOWN_TRANSITIONS = frozenset({
    "started",
    "done",
    "abandoned",
    "quarantined",
    "force-started",
    "wedged",
    "unwedged",
    "blocked",
    "unblocked",
})

# Transitions that earn a phone push by DEFAULT.
#
# `started` and `done` are deliberately excluded: they are bookkeeping -- one
# per register, one per completion -- with nothing for the operator to act
# on, and between them they burned ~31% of the Pushover account's monthly
# message allowance in Aug 2026. Their claude-events still fire unchanged;
# only the push is dropped. What remains is either an operator ask or a
# failure report.
DEFAULT_PUSH_TRANSITIONS = frozenset(KNOWN_TRANSITIONS - {"started", "done"})

# Tokens accepted in QUEUE_NOTIFY_PUSH_TRANSITIONS besides bare kinds.
_PUSH_TOKEN_ALL = "all"
_PUSH_TOKEN_NONE = "none"
_PUSH_TOKEN_DEFAULT = "default"


def _normalize_transition(raw: str) -> str:
    """Normalise one transition token: lower-cased, no ``queue `` prefix."""
    tok = (raw or "").strip().lower()
    if tok.startswith("queue "):
        tok = tok[len("queue "):].strip()
    return tok


def _push_transitions() -> frozenset:
    """Resolve the set of transitions that earn a push.

    Reads ``QUEUE_NOTIFY_PUSH_TRANSITIONS`` (comma and/or whitespace
    separated, case-insensitive). Unset or empty -> the built-in default.
    Special tokens: ``all`` (every known transition), ``none`` (no known
    transition), ``default`` (expands to the built-in set, so re-enabling a
    single transition does not mean re-listing the rest).
    """
    raw = os.environ.get("QUEUE_NOTIFY_PUSH_TRANSITIONS")
    if raw is None or not raw.strip():
        return DEFAULT_PUSH_TRANSITIONS
    out: set = set()
    for chunk in raw.replace(",", " ").split():
        tok = _normalize_transition(chunk)
        if not tok:
            continue
        if tok == "queue":
            # A bare `queue` word, left over from a space-separated
            # "queue done queue wedged" spelling. Never a transition.
            continue
        if tok == _PUSH_TOKEN_ALL:
            out |= set(KNOWN_TRANSITIONS)
        elif tok == _PUSH_TOKEN_NONE:
            continue
        elif tok == _PUSH_TOKEN_DEFAULT:
            out |= set(DEFAULT_PUSH_TRANSITIONS)
        else:
            out.add(tok)
    return frozenset(out)


def _should_push(title: str) -> bool:
    """True if this title's transition is push-worthy under current policy.

    FAILS OPEN: a title whose kind is not a KNOWN lifecycle transition (an
    ad-hoc/hand-written title, or a transition added to session-task after
    this list was written) always pushes. Suppression is only ever applied
    to kinds this file explicitly knows about.
    """
    kind = _event_kind(title)
    if kind not in KNOWN_TRANSITIONS:
        return True
    return kind in _push_transitions()



def _config_home() -> Path:
    return Path(os.environ.get("XDG_CONFIG_HOME") or (Path.home() / ".config"))


def _cache_home() -> Path:
    return Path(os.environ.get("XDG_CACHE_HOME") or (Path.home() / ".cache"))


def _parse_env_file(path: Path) -> dict:
    """Parse a tiny ``KEY="value"`` env file. No shell execution.

    Tolerates blank lines, ``#`` comments, optional ``export`` prefix, and
    single/double quoted values. Unknown lines are skipped silently.
    """
    out: dict = {}
    try:
        text = path.read_text()
    except OSError:
        return out
    for raw in text.splitlines():
        line = raw.strip()
        if not line or line.startswith("#"):
            continue
        if line.startswith("export "):
            line = line[len("export "):].lstrip()
        if "=" not in line:
            continue
        key, _, val = line.partition("=")
        key = key.strip()
        val = val.strip()
        if len(val) >= 2 and val[0] == val[-1] and val[0] in ("'", '"'):
            val = val[1:-1]
        out[key] = val
    return out


def _resolve_token() -> str | None:
    for var in ("QUEUE_NOTIFY_TOKEN", "PUSHOVER_TOKEN"):
        v = os.environ.get(var)
        if v:
            return v
    env_path = Path(
        os.environ.get("QUEUE_NOTIFY_ENV")
        or (_config_home() / "queue-notify" / "env")
    )
    return _parse_env_file(env_path).get("PUSHOVER_TOKEN") or None


def _resolve_user() -> str | None:
    # 1. Already exported.
    v = os.environ.get("PUSHOVER_USER")
    if v:
        return v
    # 2. Explicit override in the queue-notify env file.
    qn_path = Path(
        os.environ.get("QUEUE_NOTIFY_ENV")
        or (_config_home() / "queue-notify" / "env")
    )
    v = _parse_env_file(qn_path).get("PUSHOVER_USER")
    if v:
        return v
    # 3. Reuse pingme's user key (same recipient as pingme).
    pingme_path = Path(
        os.environ.get("PINGME_ENV")
        or (_config_home() / "pingme" / "env")
    )
    return _parse_env_file(pingme_path).get("PUSHOVER_USER") or None


def _post(user: str, token: str, title: str, message: str,
          priority: int, sound: str) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    fields = {
        "user": user,
        "token": token,
        "title": title,
        "message": message,
        "priority": str(priority),
        "sound": sound,
    }
    if priority == 2:
        # Emergency: required retry/expire (hourly retries for 24h).
        fields["retry"] = "3600"
        fields["expire"] = "86400"
    data = urllib.parse.urlencode(fields).encode("utf-8")
    req = urllib.request.Request(API_URL, data=data, method="POST")
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=10) as resp:
            body = resp.read().decode("utf-8", "replace")
            return (resp.status == 200, body)
    except urllib.error.HTTPError as exc:
        body = exc.read().decode("utf-8", "replace") if exc.fp else str(exc)
        return (False, body)
    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001
        return (False, f"{exc.__class__.__name__}: {exc}")


def _deliver(title: str, message: str, priority: int, sound: str) -> bool:
    """Deliver ONE push (or dry-run to the sink). Returns True on success.

    ``QUEUE_NOTIFY_SINK`` short-circuits the network POST: the composed
    delivery is appended as a JSON line to that file. This is the seam the
    test-suite uses to assert "a burst produced exactly ONE push" without
    hitting Pushover, and is handy for local debugging.
    """
    sink = os.environ.get("QUEUE_NOTIFY_SINK")
    if sink:
        record = {
            "title": title,
            "message": message,
            "priority": priority,
            "sound": sound,
            "ts": time.time(),
        }
        try:
            with open(sink, "a", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                fh.write(json.dumps(record) + "\n")
            return True
        except OSError as exc:
            print(f"queue-notify: sink write failed: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
            return False

    token = _resolve_token()
    user = _resolve_user()
    if not token or not user:
        missing = []
        if not token:
            missing.append("PUSHOVER_TOKEN (queue-notify/env)")
        if not user:
            missing.append("PUSHOVER_USER (pingme/env or queue-notify/env)")
        print(f"queue-notify: missing creds: {', '.join(missing)}",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return False

    ok, body = _post(user, token, title, message, priority, sound)
    if not ok:
        print(f"queue-notify: delivery failed: {body}", file=sys.stderr)
        return False
    return True


# --- debounce + batch spool ------------------------------------------------


def _spool_path() -> Path:
    override = os.environ.get("QUEUE_NOTIFY_SPOOL")
    if override:
        return Path(override)
    return _cache_home() / "queue-notify" / "spool.jsonl"


def _enqueue(spool: Path, record: dict) -> None:
    """Append one event record to the spool (flock-guarded)."""
    spool.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    fd = os.open(str(spool), os.O_CREAT | os.O_WRONLY | os.O_APPEND, 0o600)
    try:
        fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        os.write(fd, (json.dumps(record) + "\n").encode("utf-8"))
    finally:
        try:
            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)
        except OSError:
            pass
        os.close(fd)


def _read_records(spool: Path, truncate: bool) -> list[dict]:
    """Read all spooled records under an exclusive lock.

    With ``truncate=True`` the spool is atomically drained (read + emptied)
    so no event is double-sent. Malformed lines are skipped.
    """
    try:
        fd = os.open(str(spool), os.O_RDWR)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return []
    records: list[dict] = []
    try:
        fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        with os.fdopen(os.dup(fd), "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            for line in fh:
                line = line.strip()
                if not line:
                    continue
                try:
                    records.append(json.loads(line))
                except (ValueError, TypeError):
                    continue
        if truncate:
            os.ftruncate(fd, 0)
    finally:
        try:
            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)
        except OSError:
            pass
        os.close(fd)
    return records


def _spawn_flusher() -> None:
    """Spawn a detached flusher. Cheap: extra flushers self-exit on the lock."""
    try:
        subprocess.Popen(
            [sys.executable, os.path.abspath(__file__), "--__flush-spool"],
            stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            start_new_session=True,
            env=dict(os.environ),
        )
    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 -- never block the queue op
        print(f"queue-notify: flusher spawn failed "
              f"({exc.__class__.__name__}): {exc}", file=sys.stderr)


def _event_kind(title: str) -> str:
    """Extract the transition kind from a lifecycle title.

    Titles look like ``queue done: q-...``, ``queue force-started: q-...``,
    ``queue WEDGED: q-...``. Returns the lower-cased kind ("done",
    "force-started", "wedged", ...); falls back to "event".
    """
    head = (title or "").split(":", 1)[0].strip()
    low = head.lower()
    if low.startswith("queue "):
        head = head[len("queue "):]
    return head.strip().lower() or "event"


def _event_id(title: str) -> str:
    """Extract the queue id from a lifecycle title (text after the colon)."""
    parts = (title or "").split(":", 1)
    return parts[1].strip() if len(parts) == 2 else ""


def _compose_batch(records: list[dict]) -> tuple[str, str, int, str]:
    """Summarise ≥2 spooled events into ONE (title, message, priority, sound)."""
    n = len(records)
    counts = Counter(_event_kind(r.get("title", "")) for r in records)
    breakdown = ", ".join(
        f"{c} {k}" for k, c in sorted(counts.items(), key=lambda kv: (-kv[1], kv[0]))
    )
    host = socket.gethostname().split(".")[0]
    title = f"{host}: {n} queue events"

    lines = [f"{n} queue events: {breakdown}", ""]
    for r in records[:_BATCH_DETAIL_CAP]:
        kind = _event_kind(r.get("title", ""))
        qid = _event_id(r.get("title", ""))
        summ = (r.get("message") or "").split("\n", 1)[0].strip()
        seg = f"• {kind} {qid}".rstrip()
        if summ:
            seg += f" — {summ}"
        lines.append(seg)
    if n > _BATCH_DETAIL_CAP:
        lines.append(f"…and {n - _BATCH_DETAIL_CAP} more")

    message = "\n".join(lines)
    if len(message) > _MESSAGE_LIMIT:
        message = message[:_MESSAGE_LIMIT - 1] + "…"

    priority = max(
        (_PRIORITY_MAP.get(r.get("priority", "normal"), 0) for r in records),
        default=0,
    )
    return title, message, priority, DEFAULT_SOUND


def _deliver_records(records: list[dict]) -> None:
    """Deliver a drained batch: 1 record verbatim, ≥2 summarised into one."""
    if not records:
        return
    if len(records) == 1:
        r = records[0]
        _deliver(
            r.get("title") or socket.gethostname().split(".")[0],
            r.get("message", ""),
            _PRIORITY_MAP.get(r.get("priority", "normal"), 0),
            r.get("sound", DEFAULT_SOUND),
        )
        return
    title, message, priority, sound = _compose_batch(records)
    _deliver(title, message, priority, sound)


def _flush_spool() -> int:
    """Debounce loop: coalesce a burst, then send ONE push per batch.

    A single flusher holds an exclusive, non-blocking lock; concurrent
    invocations lose the lock and exit (the winner picks up their events).
    """
    spool = _spool_path()
    lock_path = spool.with_name(spool.name + ".flush.lock")
    try:
        lock_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    except OSError:
        pass
    lock_fd = os.open(str(lock_path), os.O_CREAT | os.O_RDWR, 0o600)
    try:
        try:
            fcntl.flock(lock_fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
        except OSError:
            return 0  # another flusher is already coalescing this burst

        debounce = _env_float("QUEUE_NOTIFY_DEBOUNCE", _DEFAULT_DEBOUNCE)
        max_window = _env_float("QUEUE_NOTIFY_MAX_WINDOW", _DEFAULT_MAX_WINDOW)
        # Poll granularity: fine enough to be responsive, coarse enough to
        # not busy-spin. Bounded by the debounce gap.
        poll_cap = max(0.1, min(debounce, 2.0))

        while True:
            records = _read_records(spool, truncate=False)
            if not records:
                return 0
            now = time.time()
            newest = max(r.get("ts", now) for r in records)
            oldest = min(r.get("ts", now) for r in records)
            quiet_for = now - newest
            age = now - oldest
            if quiet_for >= debounce or age >= max_window:
                drained = _read_records(spool, truncate=True)
                _deliver_records(drained)
                # Loop: catch stragglers that arrived during delivery.
                continue
            remaining = min(debounce - quiet_for, max_window - age)
            time.sleep(max(0.1, min(remaining, poll_cap)))
    finally:
        try:
            fcntl.flock(lock_fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)
        except OSError:
            pass
        os.close(lock_fd)


def _env_float(name: str, default: float) -> float:
    raw = os.environ.get(name)
    if raw is None or raw == "":
        return default
    try:
        val = float(raw)
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        return default
    return val if val >= 0 else default


def main(argv=None) -> int:
    if argv is None:
        argv = sys.argv[1:]

    # Hidden debounce-flusher entry point (spawned detached by _spawn_flusher).
    if argv and argv[0] == "--__flush-spool":
        return _flush_spool()

    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="queue-notify",
        description="Send a claude-watch queue push notification via Pushover.",
    )
    parser.add_argument("-p", "--priority", default="normal",
                        choices=list(_PRIORITY_MAP.keys()))
    parser.add_argument("--sound", default=DEFAULT_SOUND,
                        help=f"Pushover sound (default: {DEFAULT_SOUND}).")
    parser.add_argument("message", help="Notification body.")
    parser.add_argument("title", nargs="?", default=None,
                        help="Notification title (default: hostname).")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)

    title = args.title if args.title is not None else socket.gethostname().split(".")[0]
    priority = _PRIORITY_MAP[args.priority]

    # Transition push policy. Applied BEFORE the batch/immediate split so a
    # suppressed transition never reaches the spool and can never inflate a
    # batch's event count. Exit 0: "not push-worthy" is a policy decision,
    # not a delivery failure, and the caller (session-task) must not treat
    # it as one. The matching claude-event is emitted by session-task
    # independently and is unaffected by this.
    if not _should_push(title):
        return 0

    batch_enabled = os.environ.get("QUEUE_NOTIFY_BATCH", "1") != "0"
    # Emergency (priority 2) must never wait in a debounce window; send now.
    if not batch_enabled or priority >= _PRIORITY_MAP["emergency"]:
        ok = _deliver(title, args.message, priority, args.sound)
        return 0 if ok else 1

    # Debounce + batch: spool this event and (re)arm a detached flusher.
    try:
        _enqueue(_spool_path(), {
            "ts": time.time(),
            "priority": args.priority,
            "title": title,
            "message": args.message,
            "sound": args.sound,
        })
    except OSError as exc:
        # Spool unwritable -> fall back to an immediate send so the event is
        # never silently dropped.
        print(f"queue-notify: spool write failed ({exc}); sending immediately",
              file=sys.stderr)
        ok = _deliver(title, args.message, priority, args.sound)
        return 0 if ok else 1
    _spawn_flusher()
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
